{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/627862-revising-multiple-actions","id":627862,"title":"Revising Multiple Actions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-15T13:36:10-07:00","id":627862,"title":"Revising Multiple Actions","content":"There has long been a lot of debate over powers that provide multiple actions, especially Celerity, not only with balance but game play. I generally like splitting dice pools to perform multiple actions, but I think that it doesn't go very well with Celerity or Rage spending for multiple actions. <br /> <br /> My thought, then, was that dice splitting to attack more than once could be limited - you can only split those dice pools under special circumstances. These could include having an automatic or semiautomatic firearm, having a weapon in each hand, or using a special multi action power - like Celerity. <br /> <br /> Celerity could allow you to take as many extra actions each turn equal to your Celerity rating and to also add that score to your dice pool for multiple actions. For example, if you had a Dexterity of 3, a Melee of 3, and a Celerity of 2 - you would get 8 dice each turn that you could split among up to 3 attacks if you were using a single melee weapon. Then Celerity would require no activation and would always function.<br /> <br /> Something similar could be done with Rage or other multi action powers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T14:01:06-07:00","id":627898,"title":"","content":"I personally find multiple actions a problem in itself. They break action-economy on most games, specially when combat begins, and cWoD is no exception.<br /> <br /> Part of the problem should be solved with clearer rules about what actually takes an action. Some things could be just ruled as &quot;reflexive&quot;, or at least &quot;reflexive under circumstances&quot;. That would clean some room. Most systems today let the character take two to four actions in a turn just by assigning distinct kinds of actions to each one, and it works.<br /> <br /> Defense comes immediately to mind. I don't remember if they fixed it in the x20ths, but we still have to split dice between hitting and defending? I find it clumsy since forever...<br /> <br /> But &quot;multi-task&quot; powers giving bonuses seems a pretty good answer in my book, doesn't mater if you can split dice pool or not, so I'm with you on that. They could also add to speed when needed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T14:31:07-07:00","id":627923,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/627862-revising-multiple-actions?p=627898#post627898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I personally find multiple actions a problem in itself. They break action-economy on most games, specially when combat begins, and cWoD is no exception.<br /> <br /> Part of the problem should be solved with clearer rules about what actually takes an action. Some things could be just ruled as &quot;reflexive&quot;, or at least &quot;reflexive under circumstances&quot;. That would clean some room. Most systems today let the character take two to four actions in a turn just by assigning distinct kinds of actions to each one, and it works.<br /> <br /> Defense comes immediately to mind. I don't remember if they fixed it in the x20ths, but we still have to split dice between hitting and defending? I find it clumsy since forever...<br /> <br /> But &quot;multi-task&quot; powers giving bonuses seems a pretty good answer in my book, doesn't mater if you can split dice pool or not, so I'm with you on that. They could also add to speed when needed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I think that multiple actions are cumbersome. You could simply say that a single attack roll potentially represents multiple actions and better rolls do more damage because they might hit multiple times or with greater accuracy. <br /> <br /> I prefer static defenses myself and always have, which is unfortunate in the case of White Wolf because I don't think that the system lends itself very well to static defenses. I always have found that it detracts from the fun of combat to have to decide how well you're going to attack and sacrifice your defense to do that, I also think that it is generally unrealistic in melee combat in particular.<br /> <br /> Though within the system we have, I think that having the multi action powers work in conjunction with the dice splitting rules rather than override them might be better for balance and consistency.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T14:34:42-07:00","id":627928,"title":"","content":"Are you aware of the Dark Ages Rules to Celerity from the 20th Anniversary Dark Ages book?<br /> <br /> If not. I just fetched it from the cupboard, so: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Celerity is added to all Dexterity based dice pools, including initiative.</li> <li>It is added as one yard or meter per level to her movement.</li> <li>If a blood is spend, the vampire may change the way multiple actions are used.</li> <li>Also, the vampire my spend a blood instead to gain 1 + celerity times his normal movement as his speed this turn (lasts one turn in combat, or a scene out of combat)</li> </ol><br /> That said: how are multiple actions used: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>You must decide that you want to take multiple actions, before taking the first action.</li> <li>The first action is at a -1 dice pool penalty and a +1 difficulty. Each action there after is at an additional -1 dice pool penalty and +1 difficulty. If the difficulty would be 10 you can't take more actions. Also, only one action my be an attack.</li> </ol><br /> Celerity changes this rules if a blood is spend: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>the +1 to difficulty is not enacted, until you have had as many actions as you have Celerity. After that, the difficulty is +1 per additional action.</li> <li>The number of attacks may not be 1 + half the Celerity rating (round up).</li> </ol><br /> <br /> So, while this certainly is a much better approach to additional actions, than every edition before, I find the system still overly complicated, and it leads to certain strange situations, where the order of actions that are supposed to be happening at once are actually making a difference.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> MY APPROACH FOR A COMBAT SYSTEM<br /> I would allow a character actions, and reactions. Reactions are Dexterity + either Athletics (Dodge), Brawl (Unarmed Parry) or Melee (Armed Parray). Whenever the character is first attacked in a turn, he rolls the complete dice pool of his Reaction. For every additional time the character is attacked, the dicepool is reduced by one. Obviously not all attacks can be parried and so on.<br /> The dice pool refreshes whenever a new turn starts.<br /> <br /> Actions happen all at once. Each action has the normal dice pool, minus the additional number of actions that one takes. So if someone wants to do three things, the dice pool for all actions is -2. Movement is an action in this regard, as is the activation of powers that don't require rolls, and concentration to keep powers active, if the description state such a concentration is needed. Only one of these actions may be an attack, however (rules for double tap and the like not withstanding).<br /> <br /> Certain powers, like Celerity or Rage may allow for more than one attack, while other powers (like a certain Thauma Path) might allow for more than one power to be activated. I would keep the Celerity rules otherwise as in DA20 (with each Celerity counting as one Dexterity and the ability to spend blood to get an additional attack per uneven point).<br /> One could use Rage in a similar way, allowing the permanent rating as a bonus to attack rolls, but not to speed and dexterity rolls that aren't combat related. Also, spending one point would allow for additional attacks per uneven permanent dot.<br /> <br /> That said, I would go, as suggested by someone else, for a fixed damage based on strength, weapon used, and bonuses from gifts and the like. Damage would consist of three parts:<br /> Damage Value (the number of health lost, to be reduced by soak roll)<br /> Damage Power (a number of dice reduced from the soak pool before soak)<br /> Wound Type (if the wounds are considered fast healing, moderate healing or severe).<br /> <br /> So, combat would mean one attacks, the opponent has his reaction and if there are less successes scored, the damage is inflicted but may be soaked. The soak roll is modified by the Damage Power, but if dice remain in the dice pool, they can reduce the Damage Value.<br /> <br /> That said, Damage Values should be moderate, like Strength/2+2 for a sword or something like that. Powers, like disciplines and gifts, should only rarely change Damage Value, but on lower levels probably change Damage Power, or raise the Wound Type. Obviously, Silver should have a pretty high &quot;Damage Power&quot; against garou, but the mightiest would still have some Soak dice remain, and so on.<br /> <br /> I think such a system would all in all work well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T19:07:32-07:00","id":628168,"title":"","content":"If Fortitude just add to Soak rolls, why can't Celerity stop adding to Dex rolls?<br /> <br /> Even with only half-Celerity actions as extra attacks, you still have more attacks, and that is still the most game-breaker combat option ever in any system.<br /> <br /> For defense, I would do the simplest thing: deduce the target defensive dice pool from your attack. Its almost the same thing mathematically, unless you have different difficulties for attack and defense. If targeted more than once, the defender just loose an amount of his Defense roll for each attack, so it is hard, but not impossible, to defend from multiple assailants.<br /> <br /> That said, just add to Dex-based pools seems enough for me to all abilities that would grant extra actions. Almost all combat is dex-based anyway, and it helps to mitigate any penalty you use for multiple actions if you use them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T22:52:51-07:00","id":628235,"title":"","content":"What we've done - and this was before DAV20 came out - was handle multiple actions and Celerity in this way:<br /> <br /> 1) the character may take as many actions as she has dots in Wits or Dexterity (not both), because sometimes you're not as quick as you want to be either physically or mentally; <br /> 2) the player chooses the smallest dice pool for the actions they want to take,<br /> 3) successes are split amongst the actions. If the player wanted to take three actions and only rolled three successes, they have the option of assigning one success to each action, or two(or three) successes to one action. An action with no assigned successes is an automatic failure, <br /> 4) Celerity and other powers that grant multiple actions allow the player to utilize the full dicepool for each action. <br /> <br /> Of course, we've streamlined combat down to one roll with static everything, so combat isn't a huge time-suck for us. <br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T22:53:24-07:00","id":628236,"title":"","content":"I wonder how the math works out if celerity just added to Dex and we used the multiple action and defense rules from scion the average of dex+athletic as a static DV (though perhaps using the average of dex+wits would be more balanced) and scions very simple multiple actions rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2939-avaron","name":"Avaron","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2939_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-16T01:43:40-07:00","id":628380,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/627862-revising-multiple-actions?p=628168#post628168\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If Fortitude just add to Soak rolls, why can't Celerity stop adding to Dex rolls?</div> </div> </div> </div>That is a fair point. I think the finesse of a clock maker probably should not be increased by Celerity, but combat dice pools or initiative are fair.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/627862-revising-multiple-actions?p=628235#post628235\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What we've done - and this was before DAV20 came out - was handle multiple actions and Celerity in this way:<br /> 1) the character may take as many actions as she has dots in Wits or Dexterity (not both), because sometimes you're not as quick as you want to be either physically or mentally;<br /> 2) <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">the player chooses the smallest dice pool for the actions they want to take,</span><br /> 3) successes are split amongst the actions. If the player wanted to take three actions and only rolled three successes, they have the option of assigning one success to each action, or two(or three) successes to one action. An action with no assigned successes is an automatic failure,<br /> 4) Celerity and other powers that grant multiple actions allow the player to utilize the full dicepool for each action.<br /> <br /> Of course, we've streamlined combat down to one roll with static everything, so combat isn't a huge time-suck for us.<br /> Cheers!</div> </div> </div> </div>I always find this difficult, for actions that don't require a roll...<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-16T08:42:09-07:00","id":628691,"title":"","content":"Just ignore those actions when determining dice pool. They won't affect as they won't be affected. At most they will bring a -1 die penalty.<br /> <br /> And, curiously, there's an Elder Power, Celerity 6 or 7, called Precision, just to apply bonuses on rolls like Crafts. I always thought it cool, but too weak for its level. So... Celerity for finesse work goes well with me. Basically, you have the alacrity of mind and body to work better, even if in this case it is just you using the mind part to pay better attention at your work.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, it could give a similar bonus to Ranged Attacks based on time to aim.<br /> <br /> Never forget that, theoretically, Celerity affects perception and reason as much as it affects the body, if only because you have to coordinate your movement at such velocity. And, of course, also because it is explained as actually a bending of time, not a direct increase of muscle velocity.<br /> <br /> Similar reasoning, even if the time part isn't present, could be given to other powers of Extra Action. Rage wouldn't grant such simply because Rage wouldn't let you think clear enough to do precision stuff, anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-16T13:16:16-07:00","id":628984,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/627862-revising-multiple-actions?p=628380#post628380\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always find this difficult, for actions that don't require a roll...</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I just gave the gloss rather than the minutia, but the way we play it is that at least one success needs to be assigned to each action, even things like running or opening a door. If you want, I can link to the whole 3-page write-up for combat, multiple actions and various miscellany.<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T00:32:15-07:00","id":629668,"title":"","content":"@monteparnas<br /> Sure, Celerity could do all that, but should it do all that. The question was, if Fortitude has only a limited usage beyond Soak (namely allowing more agg to heal if the blood is used) and Potence only has limited usage, why should Celerity's Dexterity bonus be allowed for all kinds of actions?<br /> Not sure if vampire has a roll like that, but Werewolf has a gift that rolls Dex + Stealth to stand still. Celerity shouldn't help there...<br /> <br /> @nothing<br /> Three pages doesn't seem that easy a system. But sure, if you have them online.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T03:23:05-07:00","id":629781,"title":"","content":"Personally I still prefer the Revised multiple action rules, where no dice pools were split, but you simply had a dice penalty of &lt;total amount of actions&gt; for your first action, with a dice being subtracted in every additional action.<br /> <br /> So if you did two actions in one round, you had -2 dice for the first, -3 for the second.<br /> <br /> It always sounded like a more sane and simple rule to me than the more common 2nd edition and 20th anniversary multi-action rule of taking the lowest dice pool of all the actions you want to do and splitting that among the actions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T06:33:54-07:00","id":629849,"title":"","content":"I'd go for -2 for each of two actions, -3 for each of three, -4 for each of four and so on.<br /> If that is not a penalty enough, it could be -2 for each of two actions, -4 for each of three actions, -6 for each of four actions and so on.<br /> <br /> But I don't see why the penalties should rise with every action beyond the first, they should all penalize each other equally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T08:38:43-07:00","id":629967,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/627862-revising-multiple-actions?p=629668#post629668\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">@monteparnas<br /> Sure, Celerity could do all that, but should it do all that. The question was, if Fortitude has only a limited usage beyond Soak (namely allowing more agg to heal if the blood is used) and Potence only has limited usage, why should Celerity's Dexterity bonus be allowed for all kinds of actions?<br /> Not sure if vampire has a roll like that, but Werewolf has a gift that rolls Dex + Stealth to stand still. Celerity shouldn't help there...</div> </div> </div> </div>All that is still less than Extra Actions to begin with. Also, I don't think Potence and Fortitude should have limited usage.<br /> <br /> Strength have less rolls involved than Dexterity, sure. But they are key rolls. Stamina does have few usages, but that is a problem of Stamina that should be fixed anyway.<br /> <br /> The system is so bad written that theoretically equal-standing attributes in reality are completely unbalanced. But once fixed that, just making each Physical Discipline add to a Physical Attribute as its main effect would be enough, with some extras for each.<br /> <br /> But I won't bother with that here because the thread isn't about Physical Disciplines, or about Celerity to begin with. It is about Multiple Actions, and that is my thought on the matter: powers of any kind that would give Extra Actions should be rewrote as giving Dexterity bonus. Whatever effect it does have upon the Physical Disciplines balance, it is a matter of revising them. Either way Celerity is already the most powerful one at all as it is.<br /> <br /> Given that I'm prone to get rid completely of Multiple Actions in lieu of Multiple Action Types (three or four), I find <b>heinrich</b> idea the more sensible one. Use the Revised rule, but get rid of increasing difficulty once an initial penalty was determined already. In theory the Multiple Actions are occurring at roughly the same time, increasing dif even more isn't so realistic and just add cumbersome calculations during what will be an already strenuous turn-resolution, not to say that it can make some actions seemly feasible become completely unfeasible before the player realizes it. If the initial penalty isn't enough for balance, just make it greater from start, and keep the same penalty for all rolls.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T15:33:28-07:00","id":630302,"title":"","content":"I was going to link to my whole house rules/hacks n' faqs, and then I was just going to cut out and post the relevant section on multiple actions, but then I realized that between my two previous posts, I basically explained it. To sum up:<br /> <br /> Players may take a number of multiple actions equal to their Wits or Dexterity score, whichever is lower;<br /> players must choose the smaller dice pool from the actions they would like to take;<br /> players assign their successes among the actions, and every action must have a minimum of one success assigned;<br /> any actions with no assigned successes is an automatic failure, as well as any action after that in the chain;<br /> players may assign their successes in any way they like.<br /> Powers that grant multiple actions (Celerity, Rage, Time Sphere, Pandemonium, whatever Changelings have) allow the player to utilize the appropriate full dice pool for each action (though the number of actions is still limited). The actions from these powers cannot be divided.<br /> <br /> This has been working for us for about, what? Three years or so? It feels fair to the players at my table, barely takes up any more time than regular actions, and limits multiple action abuse (the largest complaint about Celerity).<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T05:05:57-07:00","id":631045,"title":"","content":"I would scrap multiple actions altogether. I'd add Celerity/Quicksilver/Rage as a bonus to Dexterity-based dicepools (and initiative) or as a multiplier to distance travelled per turn (normal distance x [1 + equivalent bonus]). To line Celerity up with Potence and Fortitude, I'd add this bonus as *dice* for free but as auto successes at the cost of one BP. Quicksilver requires a roll, so I'd make the bonus equal to the number of successes on that roll.<br /> <br /> Multiple actions really bog down combat. I would allow one attack per turn for every character, and a static defence value as a threshold to the attack roll (optionally, you could modify this by -1 for each additional attack against the target). If you want to stay close to the existing rules, just have everyone roll Dexterity + Athletics at the beginning, when they roll for initiative, and those successes can equal the defence value. You can roll the DV once per scene to cut down on rolls or just set it to half Dexterity + Athletics. The Dexterity bonus for the powers above can be added to this DV, so that, in effect, the characters are harder to hit because they're moving around at speed. This way, additional actions wouldn't be required, and additional attacks would be impossible.<br /> <br /> While fixing multiple actions, I'd probably reduce the number of rolls required in combat too. I'd make a static soak value in addition to the static DV. Bashing damage equal to or less than Stamina + Fortitude would automatically be soaked. Lethal damage equal to or less than Stamina (or half Stamina for mortals) would automatically be soaked. Anything else would require a roll.<br /> <br /> If you want to move beyond the existing rules a bit more, cWoD does already have a mechanic for factoring in static values without additional rolls. Simple make the attack roll difficulty = 2 + [defender's Dexterity + Athletics], make the damage roll difficulty = 4 + [defender's Stamina (+ any other modifiers)], and scrap soak altogether. Use the thresholds rule, so that difficulties over 9 require additional successes. Armour adds to the difficulty of the damage roll. If Athletics isn't a common trait in your games, increase the difficulty for the attack roll by 2 (so it's 4 + [defender's Dexterity + Atheltics] instead).<br /> <br /> In fact, the last mechanic is so easy I might just use that in general.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}